WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:05.600
By default, NULL values are allowed&nbsp;
in columns when tables are created.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:10.000
However, often when creating a table, one or&nbsp;
more columns should be required to have values.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:16.640
Take a look at this CREATE TABLE statement. This&nbsp;
command is similar to one we ran elsewhere in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:21.840
course, but I am now about to create a test&nbsp;
version of the BikeRepairs table we created,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:29.840
and let's look at which columns should not&nbsp;
be null. BikeRepairID should be required,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:36.480
and it should be the primary key of this table,&nbsp;
so I have added the words NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:42.160
to this column. CustomerID should not be null&nbsp;
either. The rest of these fields are debatable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:48.640
as we may not know these fields upon creating a&nbsp;
repair. One could argue that the estimated rate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:54.160
field should be required so that a customer&nbsp;
has an idea of how much a repair will cost.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.160 --> 00:01:01.040
I have added NOT NULL to that column as well.&nbsp;
I will then create this table and execute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:09.680
this command. That worked. What if, however, we&nbsp;
determine later that the estimated rate column can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:18.480
be left blank? An ALTER command will work, setting&nbsp;
the column to allow nulls. I will type the ALTER&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:33.680
TABLE command needed to do this and notice&nbsp;
that we need to specify the data type again.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:40.160
In other words, treat the syntax here as if this&nbsp;
is a brand new column even though it is not.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:50.240
I will run just this portion of the query&nbsp;
window, and we have a successful result.

